{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790995917,"nonce":"FFMWTlhP2UqCipWJkLy1sXzYT7M=","title":"FixedThreadPool dispatch and join cost dominates short jobs","message":"Reported by the ChimpMonk physics session at ash 1210987, macOS arm64.\n\nSplitting ChimpMonk's narrowphase across 4 `FixedThreadPool` workers (one `pool.run` per worker plus a `Lock` join, four dispatches per step) matched serial results but ran 2-5x slower:\n\n- 64 2D box stacks (512 pairs), 300 steps: serial 1152 ms, workers 5927 ms.\n- 256 3D stacks (1925 pairs), 60 steps: serial 1179-1480 ms, workers 1771-5036 ms.\n\nEach worker's slice is tens of microseconds, so dispatch and wake-up latency dominate. The numbers were taken under heavy machine load (another session's rustc builds at load 30-190), which inflates thread wake-up latency; re-measure on a quiet machine, and on the NUC, before drawing conclusions.\n\nRelated: the intermittent hang in the same pattern (filed separately).","files":null}]}